# Usage statistics

How much model work this project uses, split by model and by who did it (the main thread
or a subagent, and which agent type). The numbers come from the harness's own session
record; a hook copies the **counts** into the repository as the work happens, and
`bin/usage-report` summarizes them. Use them when deciding which model plays which role,
or whether a kind of work should be delegated: measured shares, not impressions.

## What is recorded, and what never is

At every end of turn (`Stop`), when a subagent finishes (`SubagentStop`){{#if harness.is_claude}} and when a session
ends (`SessionEnd`){{/if}}, `hooks/usage-log.py` reads the part of the transcript written since its
last run and appends rows to **`logs/usage.jsonl`**:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `kind` | `usage` (token counts) or `limits` (plan limits, below) |
| `ts` | time of the last response counted in the row (UTC, from the transcript) |
| `harness`, `session` | which harness, which session (a subagent carries its parent's session) |
| `role`, `agent_type` | `main` or `subagent`; for a subagent, its agent type (e.g. `worker`) |
| `model` | the model that answered |
| `calls` | API responses counted in the row, each once |
| `input` | input tokens not served from or written to the prompt cache |
| `cache_read`, `cache_write` | input tokens served from, or written to, the cache |
| `output` | output tokens, reasoning included |
| `reasoning` | reasoning tokens where the harness reports them (inside `output`; never add) |
| `peak_context` | the largest prompt of any one response in the row: how full the context was at that call (a maximum, not a sum; absent in rows written before it existed) |
| `src`, `upto` | which transcript file, and how far into it (bytes) the row reaches |

One row covers one transcript, model, role and UTC day for one run of the hook, so the
log grows by a few rows per turn. A `limits` row (Codex only) records the latest
`plan`, `primary_used_pct` / `secondary_used_pct` (share of the 5-hour and weekly windows
used), their `*_window_min` and `*_resets_at`; it is written only when a value changed.

**Never recorded:** message text, prompts, tool calls or their output, file contents,
agent briefs or descriptions. The hook reads the token-count fields and the agent type and
nothing else leaves the transcript.

**Where it lives.** `logs/usage.jsonl` is **committed**: agents add it to each commit when it
changed, as {{#if harness.coordinator_file}}{{harness.coordinator_file}}{{else}}AGENTS.md{{/if}} asks (counts only, merged by union like
the other logs). Only `logs/state/` is ignored by git: `usage-offsets.json` there remembers
how far each transcript was read. If that file is lost, the hook falls back on the
`src`/`upto` fields of the log, so nothing is counted twice.

**If the format changes.** The harnesses do not publish their transcript format. When a
transcript cannot be read (no token counts where they should be), the hook prints a
`WARNING` once per transcript and adds one `usage-format-unknown` row to
`logs/failures.jsonl`{{#if modules.reflect}}, where the next reflection pass will see it{{/if}}. It never blocks or
stops the work: on any error it exits 0 and says so on stderr. The harness usually does not
show that warning, so `bin/usage-report` repeats it above its totals ("N session record(s)
could not be read since <date>; see logs/failures.jsonl"): those sessions' tokens are
missing from the report.

## Reading it

```
bin/usage-report                      # totals by model and by role, main vs subagent share
bin/usage-report --by week            # model | role | day | week | session
bin/usage-report --since 2026-01-01   # only from that UTC day on
bin/usage-report --json               # for a script or a plot
bin/usage-report --backfill           # first import sessions the hook did not see
```

- **By role** labels each row with the tier its model belongs to (`[strong]`, `[worker]`,
  or `[other]`), matched against `[tiers]` in `project.toml`.
- **Main vs subagent share of output tokens**: output is what an agent writes. There is
  no target share: planning, proofs and anything written to the record belong on the main,
  strong model. A large main share is worth a look only when it comes from mechanical work
  the main thread did itself (long edits, routine computations) that a cheaper subagent
  could do.
- **Plan limits** (Codex) show how close the subscription windows are to full.
- **Cost.** With `[usage.prices]` in `project.toml` empty (the default) the report shows
  tokens only. Fill in your own per-model rates (US dollars per million tokens; the
  comment there shows the shape) for an estimate. On a flat-rate subscription the
  estimate measures relative weight, not money charged.

**Backfill** imports from the harness's store:
{{#if harness.is_claude}}`~/.claude/projects/<this project's path, with every non-alphanumeric character as "-">/`
(that naming is the harness's, observed rather than documented). Claude Code deletes
transcripts older than `cleanupPeriodDays` (default 30), so anything older is gone; run a
backfill soon after setting this module up if you want the earlier weeks.{{else}}`~/.codex/sessions/`, every rollout whose recorded working directory is this
project (or inside it).{{/if}} `--transcripts DIR` reads every `*.jsonl` under `DIR` instead,
in either harness's format. Rows already logged are skipped (by file name and offset), so a
backfill can be run any number of times.

## Switching it off

- For one session or command: `USAGE_LOG_OFF=1` in the environment. `HOOKS_QUIET=1` does
  **not** apply: it silences nudges, and this hook never nudges.
- For good: remove the module (its hook registrations, `hooks/usage-log.py`,
  `hooks/_usage.py`, `bin/usage-report` and this doc), or delete the hook entries from the
  harness's hook configuration. Delete `logs/usage.jsonl` too if you do not want the
  history kept.
